WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
INTRO:
We’ve all heard the narrative Apple is too big,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:11.120
Amazon is too big, Google is too big. It seems&nbsp;
like these tech giants have formed unbreakable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.000
monopolies around various segments of the market,&nbsp;
and that they’re quote on quote “too big to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.960
fail”. But, if history has taught us anything,&nbsp;
it’s that no company is too big to fail. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.240
the government and stockholders can bail out&nbsp;
a failing company and give it another 10, 20,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.120
30 years worth of lifetime, but you can&nbsp;
only do this for so long. And eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.720
these companies will fail as well. Just take a&nbsp;
look at the biggest companies throughout history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.680
Sears, Roebuck, and Co, for example, is often&nbsp;
credited with inventing the department store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.480
and throughout the 1900s, they were the biggest&nbsp;
retailer in the entire world by far. Yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:54.160
in 2018, Sears filed for bankruptcy and&nbsp;
shut down all of their stores. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.160
Chrysler and General Motors were two of the&nbsp;
biggest names in the automotive industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:04.160
throughout the 1900s, yet in the late 2000s, both&nbsp;
of them filed for bankruptcy. Now of course, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.440
guys got bailed out because they were integral to&nbsp;
the automotive industry in the late 2000s. But do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.480
you think they’ll be bailed out again if they miss&nbsp;
out on the EV transition? If there’s a handful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.320
of EV companies like Tesla, Rivian, and maybe&nbsp;
even Ford overshadowing them, I don’t think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:26.000
they’ll be bailed out. Also, if these companies&nbsp;
were truly too big to fail, they would’ve never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.600
needed to be bailed out in the first place. So&nbsp;
clearly, all companies come and go, but how does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:39.840
a company go from being an unstoppable force in&nbsp;
the market to eventually being forgotten about.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.200
PAINS OF SCALE:
Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.640
the first struggle that every large corporation&nbsp;
has to deal with is the pains of scale. We always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:52.240
hear about economics of scale, and I’m sure you&nbsp;
guys know exactly what this is. Economies of scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:57.680
is a cost advantage that large companies enjoy&nbsp;
due to serving a large market. Pains of scale or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.640
diseconomies of scale on the other hand are a cost&nbsp;
disadvantage that comes along with being too big.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.760
You see, small companies don’t have human&nbsp;
resources, or fancy corporate events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:13.520
and they don’t fly around executives and directors&nbsp;
around the world. Their employees also don’t spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:19.520
hours every day answering emails or participating&nbsp;
in meetings. In other words, smaller companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:25.440
generally have little to no operational costs.&nbsp;
YouTubers are a great example of this. While there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.160
are some creators like Mr. Beast and Good Mythical&nbsp;
Morning with massive teams and production expenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.000
behind them, most creators have extremely small&nbsp;
operational costs. PewDiePie, Sssniperwolf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.080
Athlean X, Graham Stephan, Andrei Jikh, and&nbsp;
Mark Rober are all basically just one man shows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.280
Sure, they may have a few employees that help&nbsp;
them edit and make sponsorship deals and so on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.920
but compared to their income, the amount&nbsp;
they spend on payroll is extremely small.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.800
Not to mention, most of these guys just record in&nbsp;
their bedroom or a small set inside their house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.840
So, if they have a bad month in terms&nbsp;
of views, it doesn’t really matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.120
They won’t make as much money, but they’re&nbsp;
also not in any risk of losing money. The same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.760
however, cannot be said about a channel like the&nbsp;
Infographics Show. If you were to try to replicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.480
one of their videos using professional talent,&nbsp;
it’ll easily cost you $10,000 per video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:21.680
But I’m sure they have insane economies of scale.&nbsp;
Maybe they’re cost per video is only $3000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.000
But, to get to that number, they had to take on&nbsp;
a boatload of fixed costs. In the last month, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.680
example, the Infographics Show posted 42 videos,&nbsp;
and even if each video only cost them $3000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:40.240
we’re talking about a fixed cost of about $126,000&nbsp;
per month. Given how many views the Infographics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.400
Show gets every month, I’m sure they pull&nbsp;
in way more than this in terms of revenue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:49.760
But with that being said, their operational costs&nbsp;
are a much bigger percentage of their revenue than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.640
someone like Andrei Jikh. This means that it would&nbsp;
be significantly easier for Andrei to weather a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.480
50% downturn than it would be for the Infographics&nbsp;
Show to weather a 50% downturn. This same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:04.240
principle applies to the biggest companies in the&nbsp;
world. Regardless of how the next iPhone performs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:09.440
Apple has to pay tens of millions to keep the UFO&nbsp;
in California alive, they have to pay for the tens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:14.080
of thousands of man hours spent in meetings and&nbsp;
answering emails, and they have to pay large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.640
salaries to thousands of managers and directors&nbsp;
that a smaller corporation simply wouldn’t need.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:22.960
While these may seem like negligible expenses&nbsp;
compared to Apple’s profits, the thing to note&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:28.320
is that all of these factors create friction.&nbsp;
And this friction makes Apple slower and slower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.920
which gives up and coming competitors&nbsp;
the perfect opportunity to overtake them.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.000
PROFITS OVER INNOVATION:&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.240
Aside from being a slower behemoth, larger&nbsp;
companies usually fall into the trap of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.120
prioritizing profits over innovation.&nbsp;
This is not always the company's fault&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.160
per se. The problem is often that they’ve&nbsp;
simply hit a deadend within their industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.160
I mean honestly, how much better can you make&nbsp;
an iPhone or a MacBook. Really, all that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.280
left is consistently improving the speed and&nbsp;
reliability of these devices year after year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:06.560
And as each generational jump becomes a smaller&nbsp;
and smaller difference, people upgrade less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:11.360
often which forces these companies to look for&nbsp;
alternative modes of revenue to continue growth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.800
Now, Apple has done a phenomenal job with all of&nbsp;
their services whether that be iCloud or Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:22.480
Music, but none of these are new inventions or&nbsp;
are particularly innovative. The same thing can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:27.600
said about Amazon. A lot of logistics innovation&nbsp;
had to be done to get delivery timeframes down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:33.680
from 1 week to 2 days to next day to same day to&nbsp;
1 hour delivery. But, how much more could Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:38.720
really push this, and how much would consumers&nbsp;
even care about said improvement? The truth is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:44.240
that Apple has solved the smartphone problem and&nbsp;
Amazon has solved the logistics problem. The only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:49.920
step left is to refine and maintain as long as&nbsp;
possible, but the problem is that the top talent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:55.120
is not interested in refining and maintaining. Do&nbsp;
you really think the top engineers are interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.120 --> 00:06:00.800
in making the iPhone notch smaller or removing the&nbsp;
camera bump? No, the top engineers are interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:05.200
in developing artificial intelligence, leveraging&nbsp;
machine learning, programming self-driving cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.000
creating the metaverse, launching rockets, and so&nbsp;
on and so forth. So, as larger companies start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.360
prioritize profits over innovation, the top talent&nbsp;
will slowly bleed into up and coming companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.680
In the meantime, the largest companies are&nbsp;
left with MBA businessmen who are generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.320
more interested in progressing themselves than&nbsp;
progressing the company. Now, I’m not faulting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:29.200
these guys for prioritizing self preservation over&nbsp;
pushing forward the company. But whether their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:35.280
reasoning is justified or not, in the end, the&nbsp;
company is hurt, and this leads to an extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:41.040
dangerous negative feedback loop. As employees&nbsp;
care less about the future of the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:45.760
their products become less inspiring which&nbsp;
attracts worse talent which makes their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:51.120
products even less inspiring. If this sustains&nbsp;
for long enough, you end up like JCPenney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:58.160
Wells Fargo, AT&amp;T, Dish, GE, etc. If we’re being&nbsp;
honest, none of the employees at these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.480
give a crap about the future of the&nbsp;
company. They’re all there for one reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:08.240
and one reason only: a paycheck. And when a&nbsp;
company gets to this point, it’s all but over.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:12.320
A RACE TO THE BOTTOM:&nbsp;

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:17.200
When all the innovation within an industry&nbsp;
is gone, what follows next is always a race&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:22.320
to the bottom. In the automotive industry, for&nbsp;
example, once most of the innovation was done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:28.560
Toyota and Honda stepped in and made cars cheaper&nbsp;
and more affordable than ever before. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.800
once the airline deregulation act of 1978 passed,&nbsp;
airline ticket prices fell off a cliff. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.400
quite natural. As innovation slows down, a given&nbsp;
product or service loses much of its appeal, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:46.320
company’s fanbases dissipate. This isn’t to say&nbsp;
that people no longer use said product or service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:51.760
but simply that they no longer take pride in a&nbsp;
certain brand. For example, in the late 1800s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:56.080
a wealthy gentleman may have been proud to&nbsp;
get their electricity from the best provider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:02.960
in the nation. Today, electricity is used more&nbsp;
than ever before, but no one gives a crap about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:08.160
who their electricity provider is. Electricity is&nbsp;
just a part of daily life and people choose their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:13.680
provider based on who’s the cheapest not what&nbsp;
brand it’s from. Similarly, when was the last time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.960
you cared about where your gas comes from or where&nbsp;
your steel came from? Even if you do care about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:24.080
these factors, it’s likely because you care about&nbsp;
the product itself as opposed to the company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:29.040
Maybe you only buy your steel and gas from one&nbsp;
company because you need a certain quality of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:35.120
steel or you want the highest quality of gas. But,&nbsp;
in these scenarios, you’re not loyal to the brand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:40.080
you’re loyal to the product. If a new gas company&nbsp;
came out tomorrow and offered a better quality of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:45.360
gas for the same price, you’d likely switch in&nbsp;
a heartbeat. Clearly, the same thing cannot be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:51.600
said about iPhones and Androids or Mac OS and&nbsp;
Windows. These inventions are still rather new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:56.960
and people are still extremely divided on these&nbsp;
subjects. But with time, people will care less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:02.000
and less as these products basically become the&nbsp;
same thing. Something else to note is that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:07.040
of these companies have already experienced this&nbsp;
in the past. Apple, for example, nearly went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:12.160
bankrupt in the late 1990s after the initial PC&nbsp;
boom came to an end and budget options flooded the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:18.000
market. They had to pivot to the mobile market to&nbsp;
reignite growth. Similarly, Microsoft had a decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:23.840
of stagnation after 2000 as they had already won&nbsp;
the PC market. It wasn’t until Microsoft embraced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:28.880
the cloud and servers that they started to grow&nbsp;
once again. If Microsoft and Apple hadn’t made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:35.840
these shifts, it’s likely that they would’ve&nbsp;
already gone bankrupt or ended up like IBM.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:37.600
30 YEARS:&nbsp;

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:42.880
I know a lot of people would like to see Amazon&nbsp;
go bankrupt, but what’s ironic is that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:49.360
person who’s most confident that Amazon will go&nbsp;
bankrupt is Jeff Bezos. In 2018, Jeff said quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:56.080
“Amazon is not too big to fail ... In fact,&nbsp;
I predict one day Amazon will fail. Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:00.960
will go bankrupt. If you look at large companies,&nbsp;
their lifespans tend to be 30-plus years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:05.920
not a hundred-plus years." And if we think about&nbsp;
it, many of the big tech companies that people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:12.960
complain about are even 30 years old. Amazon,&nbsp;
Google, Tesla, Nvidia, and Facebook are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:18.800
less than 30 years old. The two big tech companies&nbsp;
that are older than that are Apple and Microsoft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.840
and as we previously touched on, the only reason&nbsp;
these guys are still crushing it is because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:29.600
basically became a new business right around that&nbsp;
30 year mark. Meanwhile, Amazon and Google are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:34.880
fast approaching that 30 year mark, and likewise&nbsp;
they’ve basically reached market saturation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:40.800
their main industries and they’re facing more&nbsp;
backlash than ever before. Similarly, Facebook may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:46.560
be the most hated company in the world, and they&nbsp;
haven’t even been around for 20 years. This isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:52.160
to say any of these companies are gonna collapse&nbsp;
anytime soon, but this next decade could very well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:58.080
be the largest and most dominant that you’ll ever&nbsp;
see them. After all, it’s just a matter of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:02.240
until a company’s friction builds up, they&nbsp;
start to prioritize profits over innovation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:06.800
they lose their top talent, cheaper alternatives&nbsp;
enter the market, the customer base dissipates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:38.400
and the company becomes a shell of their former&nbsp;
self. And I think Jeff Bezos says it best. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:44.000
do you guys think Apple will file for bankruptcy?&nbsp;
Comment that down below. Also, drop a like if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:48.480
hate bailouts. And of course, consider checking&nbsp;
out our international channels to watch our videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:52.480
in other languages and consider subscribing&nbsp;
